    <h1>Fan-tailed Raven vs Brown-necked Raven: How to Tell Them Apart</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Fan-tailed Raven is smaller and shorter-tailed, with a rounded "fan" tail shape visible in flight that is distinctive once learned. The Brown-necked Raven is larger with a proportionally longer, more graduated tail. The Fan-tailed's bill is also shorter and less strongly arched. Both are mostly all-black desert corvids; the brownish neck tone of the Brown-necked is subtle and variable.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Fan-tailed Raven</th><th>Brown-necked Raven</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Scientific name</td><td><em>Corvus rhipidurus</em></td><td><em>Corvus ruficollis</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Body length</td><td>44–47 cm</td><td>50–53 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tail shape</td><td>Short, broad, rounded — fan-shaped in flight</td><td>Longer, graduated; not fan-shaped</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bill</td><td>Shorter, less deeply curved</td><td>Longer, more arched</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wing shape in flight</td><td>Shorter, broader wings; more buoyant flight</td><td>Longer wings; more direct flight</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Neck/nape</td><td>Glossy black throughout</td><td>Brownish dull cast on neck/nape (variable, light-dependent)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call</td><td>Short, deep "korr" or nasal "aak"</td><td>Deep, croaking "kraa" or "kronk"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Preferred habitat</td><td>Rocky mountains, cliffs, gorges</td><td>Flat desert, semi-desert, stony plains</td></tr>
        <tr><td>IUCN status</td><td>Least Concern</td><td>Least Concern</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Tail — Key Character in Flight</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Fan-tailed Raven gets its name from the distinctive broad, rounded tail that fans out visibly in flight. While all raven tails look "wedge" or "diamond" shaped compared to crows, the Fan-tailed Raven's tail is proportionally shorter and much broader, creating an almost circular silhouette when fanned. This is visible from a reasonable distance and is the most reliable single character when good views are obtained in flight.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The Brown-necked Raven has a more graduated, longer tail — still diamond-shaped but more elongated, closer in proportion to other large ravens.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Size and Structure</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Brown-necked Raven is appreciably larger — the size difference is noticeable when both birds are seen together. When alone, comparison with other species in the landscape helps. The Fan-tailed Raven also has a relatively shorter, stouter bill. Both have the heavy, arched corvid bill profile, but the Brown-necked's is proportionally longer.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Brown Neck — How Useful Is It?</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Brown-necked Raven's scientific name (<em>ruficollis</em>, "red-necked" or "rufous-necked") refers to the brownish-dull neck feathers that distinguish it from the iridescent gloss of fully black corvids. In practice, this is a character best seen in good light at close range. In harsh desert light, against bright sky, or at distance, the brownish neck can be invisible. Field guides advise treating the neck colour as a supporting character rather than a primary identification feature.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

